    <h1 class="section-heading">TinyML Asia 2020: My Takeaways</h1>
    <p class="post-date article-date">November 20, 2020</p>
    <p class="tag-list article-tags"><span class="tag">TinyML</span><span class="tag">Conference notes</span></p>
    <p>The following are my takeaways from attending the TinyML Asia Summit during November 16–19, 2020.</p>
    <p>First, it was great to have the option to attend the virtual conference for free. I had only recently become interested in the topic, and having such a low barrier to gaining a tonne of new information was amazing. Winning a $30 Starbucks gift card didn’t hurt either.</p>
    <p>The conference was a week of thematically linked talks covering hardware, algorithms, systems, and applications.</p>
    <h2>My takeaways</h2>
    <p>If I had to identify one major trend in TinyML, it would be the strong emphasis on hardware–software co-design. This is natural, given that many of the abstraction layers we see today emerged as computers gained increasing resources.</p>
    <p>TinyML applications have to be as lightweight as possible because power and energy are precious resources. This means simpler hardware, with much of the complexity handled by compilers and software.</p>
